1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,020
Speaker 1: Hi everybody, Peter Zion coming to you from Washington, D.C. That's Mellon Auditorium behind me.

2
00:00:04,220 --> 00:00:08,960
Speaker 1: Today we're going to talk about an up-to-coming trip that Joe Biden has to Angola.

3
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:14,480
Speaker 1: Now, during the Cold War, the United States and Angola were definitely on opposite sides.

4
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:20,700
Speaker 1: There was a civil war there that the Soviets and the Cubans and later the Chinese were supporting on one side.

5
00:00:20,820 --> 00:00:25,560
Speaker 1: We were supporting on the—oh, hello, that's a big-ass squirrel that seems to think that I have food for it.

6
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:31,260
Speaker 1: Huh. Anyway, during that time, so the Americans were on one side, the Soviets were on the other side,

7
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:37,000
Speaker 1: and American oil companies were helping the Soviet-backed government generate oil that was then sold to Europe,

8
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:38,780
Speaker 1: and, you know, Cold War, weird stuff.

9
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:44,740
Speaker 1: Anyway, bottom line is that the Cold War is long since over, Americans are saying bygones,

10
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:49,940
Speaker 1: and the Angolans are a little curious as to the details, but they're open to some sort of a deal.

11
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:55,360
Speaker 1: It's not that Angola is all that important to the United States for its own sake.

12
00:00:55,360 --> 00:00:58,400
Speaker 1: I mean, yes, they produce one and a half to two million barrels of crude a day,

13
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:02,960
Speaker 1: but the U.S. is the world's largest refined product exporter now and the world's largest crude producer,

14
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:06,560
Speaker 1: so it's not like we need it for us or even for our allies anymore.

15
00:01:07,060 --> 00:01:09,280
Speaker 1: The issue has to do with mining.

16
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:15,680
Speaker 1: Africa is kind of the great frontier for large-scale mining,

17
00:01:15,820 --> 00:01:19,240
Speaker 1: particularly on a belt of countries going from Congo south.

18
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:21,920
Speaker 1: This is the old Cecil Rhodes route.

19
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:25,360
Speaker 1: Cecil Rhodes is the guy who basically founded modern South Africa.

20
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:32,340
Speaker 2: And from the Copper Belt in southern Congo, there's a series of collection railroads that link together and form a spine

21
00:01:32,340 --> 00:01:39,500
Speaker 2: going down Zambia, Zimbabwe, Botswana, and ultimately reaching the better ports in South Africa.

22
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:48,680
Speaker 3: Well, there's something called the Benguela Roadway or the Lobito Corridor that cuts across Angola to the Atlantic that intersects this line.

23
00:01:49,180 --> 00:02:02,660
Speaker 2: And ever since apartheid ended in South Africa, the government has become increasingly dysfunctional and the maintenance on the main spine railway has steadily degraded to the point that it's pretty rough in a lot of places.

24
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:12,700
Speaker 2: So, the idea the United States has is if we can rehabilitate the Lobito Corridor and rebuild the Benguela Railway, which dates back to the Portuguese occupation a century ago,

25
00:02:13,220 --> 00:02:20,940
Speaker 2: wow, then there's another route for this stuff to get out, and it would be going to the Atlantic instead of the Indian Ocean Basin,

26
00:02:20,960 --> 00:02:24,660
Speaker 2: and that's closer to the United States and Europe as opposed to China.

27
00:02:24,660 --> 00:02:32,720
Speaker 2: So, it's become a bit of a tug of war that the Angolans are encouraging because everybody's spending infrastructure money in their country.

28
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:36,960
Speaker 2: And the people who won the Civil War, the pro-Soviet, are a minority.

29
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:43,720
Speaker 2: So, now we have the group that the United States used to support, which is closer to the majority, that's kind of an oppressed population.

30
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:49,840
Speaker 2: So, once again, there's all kinds of weird geopolitics going on in this southwest African nation.

31
00:02:50,920 --> 00:02:53,420
Speaker 2: At this point, building the railway is pretty straightforward.

32
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:58,800
Speaker 2: The United States is basically invested in this project as one of its bigger overseas aid projects,

33
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,480
Speaker 2: and it's probably going to be completed and operational within a couple of years.

34
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:03,660
Speaker 2: Too little, too late.

35
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:04,860
Speaker 2: Time will tell.

36
00:03:05,380 --> 00:03:07,680
Speaker 2: But the country is very much in play.

37
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:09,360
Speaker 2: And so, of course, Uncle Joe's going there.
